mandiant.tsv.gz: what Mandiant's reader (macos-UnifiedLogs'
unifiedlog_iterator v0.7.0, x86_64 Linux release binary, SHA-256
e43dff279d16c96a00b6de0c16a2385bb287f3d6ae257852d6f216f0c907bf8c) gives for
the fixtures in tests/fixtures/mandiant/, one line per entry: file, time
(nanoseconds, rounded from Mandiant's floating point), thread, pid, euid,
event type, log type, activity and parent activity, subsystem, category,
process and its UUID, library and its UUID, format string, message. Tabs,
newlines, carriage returns and backslashes in values are escaped (\t \n \r
\\). make_fixtures.py made the fixtures and this file, on Linux, from
Mandiant's v1.0.0 test_data.zip:

  python3 -I make_fixtures.py test_data unifiedlog_iterator /tmp/work REPO

It cuts each fixture down (see tests/fixtures/mandiant/NOTICE) and checks
that Mandiant's reader gives the same output for the cut-down archive as
for the full one (protocol buffer statedumps aside: Mandiant writes their
fields in hash map order, which changes from run to run, so a new run
reorders those few messages' fields; the fixtures come out the same).

apple.tsv.gz: what Apple's `log show --style ndjson --info --debug
--signpost` (macOS 26) gives for the same fixtures, one line per entry:
fixture, thread, continuous time, event type, format string, message,
signpost name. apple.py wrote it, on macOS:

  python3 -I apple.py REPO /tmp/work

lzbitmap.py rewrote big_sur/Special/0000000000000001.tracev3's chunksets with
LZBITMAP, with macOS's libcompression, into
tests/fixtures/mandiant/big_sur/lzbitmap/0000000000000001.tracev3 (then
gzip -9n):

  python3 -I lzbitmap.py in.tracev3 out.tracev3

tests/oracle.rs compares this crate's reading of the fixtures with both
files, and, when asked, a whole archive with Mandiant's JSON lines and
Apple's ndjson (see its documentation).
